f":"Client approved"},"cognitive_decision_step":{"action":"Completed site survey and zoning report assessing the constrained affordable-housing parcel.","artifact":"Sun studies","rationale":{"initial":"Initial approach based on vagueness in requirements","improved":"Refined approach after considering tight timeline"}},"phase_outcome":{"completion_status":"Completed","deliverables_produced":["Site survey","Context analysis","Sun studies","Zoning report"],"progress_percentage":75,"key_decisions":["Decision based on Vagueness","Resolved through Applied to Site Analysis in Architecture context"],"iteration_count":5,"stakeholder_approval":"Approved after iterations"}},{"phase_number":2,"phase_name":"Concept Development","complexity":"High","cognitive_state":"Vagueness","requirements":["Explore budget-efficient layout concepts","Study compact repeatable unit types","Sketch cost-driven massing options"],"duration_estimate":41,"think_aloud":{"phase_context":"Phase 2: Zoning Review.","cognitive_state":"Vagueness","utterance":"Site's surveyed, footprint's pinned. Now budget-conscious concepts. Efficient — the dial's the circulation. A double-loaded corridor gets me maybe 82 percent net-to-gross; push to a point-block core and it's 85 but the units get deeper, darker. Where's affordable actually sit? Hold the double-loaded corridor, keep units shallow enough for daylight. Study the compact repeatable type — one bay, flippable. Sketch the cost-driven massing, simple rectangle, no expensive geometry. Iterations logged. Concepts sketched.","continuity":" Transitioning from Uncertainty in previous phase.","style":"explorer","generation_method":"framework_based","framework_influenced":true},"behaviors":["create_multiple_variations","visual_experimentation","broad_exploration","abductive_leap","frame_creation"],"ai_usage":{"used":true,"tools":[{"tool":"Claude","purpose":"Design documentation","interaction_type":"Technical writing","satisfaction":4},{"tool":"Figma AI","purpose":"UI component generation","interaction_type":"Design system automation","satisfaction":4}],"purpose":"Concretizing vague requirements and finding specific examples","effectiveness":0.8458382929564016,"reasoning":"Leveraging AI to clarify fuzzy requirements and generate concrete examples from vague descriptions"},"outcomes":{"design_quality":70.72777559781755,"cognitive_load":8.812684517671105,"satisfaction":5,"quality":4,"innovation":5,"efficiency":3},"actual_duration":33.5,"phase_insights":["High cognitive load - challenging phase","Quality improvement from previous phase"],"framework_approach":"Creates entirely new frames for understanding problems","cognitive_style":"transformative","ultra_realistic_think_aloud":"Site's surveyed, footprint's pinned. Now budget-conscious concepts. Efficient — the dial's the circulation. A double-loaded corridor gets me maybe 82 percent net-to-gross; push to a point-block core and it's 85 but the units get deeper, darker. Where's affordable actually sit? Hold the double-loaded corridor, keep units shallow enough for daylight. Study the compact repeatable type — one bay, flippable. Sketch the cost-driven massing, simple rectangle, no expensive geometry. Iterations logged. Concepts sketched.","enhanced_requirements":["Develop concept development based on previous phase for Architecture","Consider tight timeline for Architecture","Iterate based on feedback for Architecture"],"original_requirements":["Develop concept development based on previous phase","Consider tight timeline","Iterate based on feedback"],"cognitive_state_rationale":"Key terms in Zoning Review lack precise definitions or measurable criteria","design_context":{"current_challenge":"Transforming fuzzy requirements into concrete design decisions","design_approach":"Creates entirely new frames for understanding problems using transformative thinking","key_constraints":["Timeline: 41h allocated","Complexity: High","Dependencies: Technical specs"],"success_criteria":["Design principles maintained","Technical feasibility confirmed","Innovative solution delivered"]},"metadata":{"phase_index":2,"total_phases":8,"domain":"Architecture","project_name":"Residential Building Design","designer":"Designer_49","timestamp":"2024-04-17"},"deliverables":["Research documents","Design iterations","Review feedback","Final deliverables"],"outcome_summary":["High quality output achieved","Innovative solutions developed","Delivered within timeline","Stakeholders highly satisfied"],"completion_status":{"status":"Approved","completion_date":"2024-02-16","sign_off":"Client approved"},"cognitive_decision_step":{"action":"Sketched efficient, budget-conscious concepts for quality affordable homes for Cedarline Housing.","artifact":"Design iterations","rationale":{"initial":"Initial approach based on vagueness in requirements","improved":"Refined approach after considering unclear requirements"}},"phase_outcome":{"completion_status":"Completed","deliverables_produced":["Research documents","Design iterations","Review feedback","Final deliverables"],"progress_percentage":25,"key_decisions":["Decision based on Vagueness","Resolved through Applied to Concept Development in Architecture context"],"iteration_count":2,"stakeholder_approval":"Approved after iterations"}},{"phase_number":3,"phase_name":"Schematic Design","complexity":"High","cognitive_state":"Ambiguity","requirements":["Lay out efficient compact unit plans","Set economical stacking and massing","Optimize circulation-to-unit ratio"],"duration_estimate":40,"think_aloud":{"phase_context":"Phase 1: Site Analysis.","cognitive_state":"Uncertainty","utterance":"Concepts are sketched — compact type, plain massing. Now schematic plans. Efficient unit — that could mean smallest floor area to code, or best-value layout where a slightly bigger plan sells better. Two readings pulling apart. On this budget I optimize the ratio, not the minimum: hit the 50 square metre one-bed cleanly, no wasted corridor. Lay out the compact plans, set the economical stack, tune circulation-to-unit. Massing model up. Schematic set standing after all these years, still comes down to that ratio.","continuity":"","style":"explorer","generation_method":"framework_based","framework_influenced":true},"behaviors":["prepare_multiple_scenarios","hypothesis_testing","parallel_prototyping","paradigm_shift_thinking","abductive_leap"],"ai_usage":{"used":true,"tools":[{"tool":"Claude","purpose":"Design critique and feedback","interaction_type":"Analytical dialogue","satisfaction":5},{"tool":"Stable Diffusion","purpose":"Rapid prototyping visuals","interaction_type":"Local generation","satisfaction":4}],"purpose":"Researching data, analyzing trends, and gathering information","effectiveness":0.8002004449928837,"reasoning":"Utilizing AI to gather missing information and explore potential scenarios under uncertainty"},"outcomes":{"design_quality":62.60941896199791,"cognitive_load":10.88422929720667,"satisfaction":5,"quality":3,"innovation":3,"efficiency":3},"actual_duration":35.4,"phase_insights":["High cognitive load - challenging phase"],"framework_approach":"Creates entirely new frames for understanding problems","cognitive_style":"transformative","ultra_realistic_think_aloud":"Concepts are sketched — compact type, plain massing. Now schematic plans. Efficient unit — that could mean smallest floor area to code, or best-value layout where a slightly bigger plan sells better. Two readings pulling apart. On this budget I optimize the ratio, not the minimum: hit the 50 square metre one-bed cleanly, no wasted corridor. Lay out the compact plans, set the economical stack, tune circulation-to-unit. Massing model up. Schematic set standing after all these years, still comes down to that ratio.","enhanced_requirements":["Understand innovative design solution for Architecture","Address tight timeline for Architecture","Define project scope and constraints for Architecture"],"original_requirements":["Understand innovative 