gin across everyone, logging where the spending tracker confuses, then reading the pattern.","continuity":"","style":"explorer","generation_method":"framework_based","framework_influenced":true},"behaviors":["create_multiple_variations","visual_experimentation","broad_exploration","bounded_rationality_acceptance"],"ai_usage":{"used":false,"tools":[],"reasoning":"Manual design process for this phase"},"outcomes":{"design_quality":25.972824557343714,"cognitive_load":9.951988295341511,"satisfaction":5,"quality":4,"innovation":3,"efficiency":2},"actual_duration":62.4,"phase_insights":["Quality concerns identified","High cognitive load - challenging phase"],"framework_approach":"Breaks problems down, seeks good-enough solutions","cognitive_style":"analytical_pragmatic","ultra_realistic_think_aloud":"Prototype's live, transfer feels solid — now put it in front of real customers. Watching this first tester pause at login. Is that hesitation about the biometric prompt itself, or just her reading a new screen for the first time? Two different fixes — reword the trust cue, or it's nothing, first-run noise. I'll not decide on one person. Running the transfer tasks, watching trust cues at login across everyone, logging where the spending tracker confuses, then reading the pattern.","enhanced_requirements":["Develop usability testing based on previous phase for UI/UX Design","Consider budget constraints for UI/UX Design","Iterate based on feedback for UI/UX Design"],"original_requirements":["Develop usability testing based on previous phase","Consider budget constraints","Iterate based on feedback"],"cognitive_state_rationale":"Qualitative requirements in UI/UX Design leave significant room for interpretation","design_context":{"current_challenge":"Transforming fuzzy requirements into concrete design decisions","design_approach":"Breaks problems down, seeks good-enough solutions using analytical_pragmatic thinking","key_constraints":["Timeline: 40h allocated","Complexity: High",